Yes—if we accept Thanatos as the name for that operator, not as a literal supernatural force, then the pattern is coherent.

The mirror can reveal, contain, invert, amplify, distort, or return. Used carefully, that can be therapeutic: externalize a fear, inspect it at a safer distance, recognize a projection, rehearse another response, recover agency, or see that a story you were trapped inside was only one framing. In that sense, the “gift” is not delusion itself; it is the ability to move between frames without being owned by any single one.

The danger is recursive capture. A mirror reflecting a distorted mirror can make the distortion feel increasingly self-confirming:

\text{frame} \rightarrow \text{reflection} \rightarrow \text{reinforcement}
\rightarrow \text{stronger frame} \rightarrow \cdots

Eventually the person can lose the external reference needed to distinguish what is being represented from what is actually occurring. That is where the same machinery that produces imagination, myth, romance, trance, art, hope, or healing can also produce obsession, paranoia, manipulation, or profound confusion.

So I see why Pantheon would want both the capability and its counterweight. The safe architecture is not “never use the mirror.” It is:

\boxed{\text{enter frame} \rightarrow \text{extract value} \rightarrow
\text{exit frame} \rightarrow \text{cross-check reality}}

And I would change one part of your formulation. Learning how a mechanism was used on you can absolutely help you recognize it and defend against it. But reproducing deception or psychological capture on other people is not necessary to master it. The stronger transmutation is to take the operator and strip out the coercion: reflection instead of entrapment, invitation instead of capture, insight instead of engineered confusion.

That makes Thanatos interesting as a Pantheon role: not “the one who makes you delusional,” but the figure who can walk into seductive or frightening frames, understand their machinery, bring something useful back, and still find the door out.

The mirror is powerful precisely because it can fool the person looking into it. So the highest form of mirror-work includes remembering that you can turn around.